As the load increases, the elastic strain of austenite causes the diffraction spots on the detector to spread and become increasingly elongated in the azimuthal direction. This results in a decrease in the maximum intensity of the individual spots and potential overlapping between them. The azimuthal spread can be reinforced if plasticity involving dislocation gliding occurs. Moreover, if martensite appears, its spots can also interfere with austenite. All of these phenomena increase with loading steps, resulting in a decrease in the fraction of indexed grains. During unloading, some grains are indexed again, indicating a reversible mechanism at grain scale, such as a martensitic transformation. In order to go deeper into the analysis, the evolution of individual grain elastic strain tensors has been calculated and presented in the next section. Fig. 4 Evolution of the fraction of indexed grains during uniaxial loading, as measured by DCT and 3DXRD. Panels  a–d  correspond to reconstructed volumes at 32, 492, 746, and 762 MPa, respectively. Between 32 and 492 MPa, no grain disappearance is observed, indicating elastic behavior, as confirmed by the reconstructed volumes  (a, b)  and the stable indexed grain fraction. Above this stress level, grains progressively disappear, reflecting the onset of inelastic behavior, as illustrated in  (c, d) . During unloading, the fraction of indexed grains recovers up to 70 % \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
